/**
\page org_mitk_views_agent The Agent

The Agent view is a chat with an AI agent that works in the Workbench for you. You describe what you
want, for example "segment the liver in this CT and report its volume", and watch the Workbench while
the agent does it: it opens views, changes how data is displayed, segments, measures and writes
reports. The agent is your own installation of Claude Code; the Workbench starts it in the background
and gives it a set of tools that reach the Workbench through its REST API.

What the agent does is the work of a language model. Look at its results as you would at those of a
colleague you do not know yet: check them, and see the section \ref org_mitk_views_agent_limits.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_requirements Requirements and sign-in

The agent needs Claude Code on your computer, and an account that Claude Code signs in with: a Claude
subscription, an Anthropic Console account, or an API key. The Workbench does not install Claude
Code and never sees your credentials; signing in is done by Claude Code itself.

The line at the top of the view tells where you stand. If Claude Code was not found, or you are not
signed in, it offers a link that opens the preferences.

\subsection org_mitk_views_agent_preferences Preferences

The preference page "Agent" has these settings:

- <b>Claude Code</b> is the program the agent runs on. Leave it empty to look for it on the PATH and in
  <tt>~/.local/bin</tt>, where its installer puts it. "Browse..." chooses another file.
- The <b>Sign-in</b> box shows how Claude Code signs in now. "Sign In with a Claude Subscription..." and
  "Sign In with an Anthropic Console Account..." start the sign-in of Claude Code, which continues in
  your web browser; if no browser opens, the dialog offers the address of the sign-in page, and if the
  page shows a code, you paste it into the dialog. "Sign Out"
  ends the sign-in with a subscription or a Console account. It does not remove an API key that is set
  in the environment.
- <b>Ignore ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</b>: Claude Code prefers an API key in the environment variable
  <tt>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</tt> over a Claude subscription, and the key is billed per token. If you want
  the agent to use your subscription although the variable is set, check this box: the agent does not
  get the variable then.

\subsection org_mitk_views_agent_cost Cost

A Claude subscription is not charged per token, but the conversation counts toward its usage limits.
With an API key or a Console account, usage is billed per token. Under the conversation, the view shows
"Conversation: $x", what the conversation would cost with an API key, and each answer ends with its own
"API-equivalent cost". These are the only figures the view shows; what you actually pay or use up is
told by your account.

The model and the effort that the agent works with are chosen below the input. More effort takes longer
and uses more of the usage limits. A change applies from the next message on, and the conversation
continues.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_data What leaves the computer

The agent is a language model that does not run on your computer. What it reads goes to the provider of
the account that Claude Code is signed in with, as in any session of Claude Code:

- your messages,
- what the agent asks the Workbench for, and gets: the names and properties of data, measurements,
  statistics, DICOM tags and the like, and
- the screenshots of the render windows that it takes to look at the images.

The images themselves and the files on your computer stay where they are: the agent cannot read files,
and it has no shell and no access to the internet. It sees an image only through the screenshots and the
numbers that it asks for. Whatever is visible in a screenshot, or written in a name or a DICOM tag,
leaves the computer if the agent asks for it, so do not use the agent on data that must not be sent to
the provider of your account.

The notes the agent keeps (see \ref org_mitk_views_agent_memory) are sent at the start of each
conversation as well.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_server The REST API server

The agent works through the REST API of the Workbench, which the REST API view monitors. If no server is
running when the view starts, the Agent view starts one for itself. It listens on this computer only,
on a port that the system chooses, requires a token, and gives clients no access to your files. It is
stopped when the Agent view closes. The token is created for the agent alone and handed to Claude Code
in a configuration file in the agent's workspace folder; it is removed when the view closes.

If a server is already running, for example one that you started in the REST API view, the Agent view
uses it, with a token of its own and without access to your files. If that server is open to others,
the view first tells you how, and asks whether to continue:

- Programs on this computer can use it without a token, and web pages in your browser can use parts
  of it.
- Other computers can reach it.
- These clients can load any file you can read into the Workbench.

If you continue, others can use the server while the agent works, as they could before. "Do not show
again for these risks" remembers your answer for these risks. If you cancel, the agent does not work in
the Workbench for now. To let the Agent view start a server of its own, stop the running one, or let it
require a token and restart it, and then start a new conversation. If the server is restarted with
other settings or stopped while the view is open, reopen the Agent view.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_lock While the agent works

While the agent works, the Workbench is locked so that you and the agent do not change the same things:

- Views and editors do not react to the mouse or the keyboard, but keep showing what happens. Their
  tabs show a lock instead of their icon; its tool tip says why.
- Undo, Redo and Close Project are disabled.
- Menus and tool bars otherwise stay usable, as do parts that do not follow the lock, like the Welcome
  page.
- The Agent view stays usable. Its Memory button, the model and the effort are disabled meanwhile.

The lock ends when the agent has finished its answer. You can end it earlier:

- <b>Stop</b> interrupts the agent. Its calls to the Workbench are refused from then on, and the view
  shows "Stopping".
- If the agent does not stop, <b>Stop</b> a second time ends Claude Code. Your next message continues
  the conversation.
- <b>New conversation</b> ends the conversation and starts a new one, in which the agent does not know
  the earlier messages. The conversation on screen is cleared, and so is its cost.

If you close the Agent view or the Workbench while the agent works, you are asked whether to stop it.
Between its answers the Workbench is yours: change what you like. The agent is told to look at the
current state again at the start of each turn.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_conversation The conversation

Your messages and the agent's answers appear in the conversation. Enter sends a message,
Shift+Enter starts a new line. Links in the answers open in your web browser.

The work the agent does between your message and its answer is shown as steps. By default, each run of
steps is one line, for example "12 steps", that you can click to show the steps with their results. The
check box "Show steps" shows them all the time. Screenshots that the agent took appear in the steps as
small images; click one to see it larger in a window of its own.

An answer ends with a note, for example "Done in 42.0 s (API-equivalent cost $0.35)", or with "Stopped."
or "Failed." If Claude Code cannot continue the conversation, the view says so; your next message
then starts a new conversation, in which the agent does not know the earlier messages, and you have to
send the message again.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_memory Memory

The agent can save short notes about how the Workbench works, which it learned from you or by trying,
so that it does not have to find it out again. It gets all notes at the start of every conversation.
It is told not to save patient data, image contents or findings. Each note it saves or deletes is
mentioned in the steps.

The Memory button shows how many notes there are:

- "Show..." opens a dialog with the notes. Double-click a note to edit it; "Delete" removes the selected
  note.
- "Clear..." deletes all notes after a question.

A note has at most 500 characters, and there are at most 50 notes. Changes apply from the next
message on. The notes are kept in the file <tt>memory.json</tt> in the Agent folder of the Workbench's
local application data. A note can be wrong or outdated; delete those that are.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_does What the agent can do

The agent uses what the REST API of the Workbench offers. It is told to look an endpoint up before it
uses it, so it can do whatever the API offers, among other things:

- list, show and hide data and change its properties, for example opacity, color and contrast,
- open and close views, scroll through the slices of the render windows, zoom, turn the 3D window and
  change the layout,
- segment with nnInteractive, VoxTell and TotalSegmentator, manage the labels and groups of a
  segmentation, and split a label into its components (as the Segmentation view does),
- measure the labels, compute statistics and draw and measure planar figures (as the Measurement and
  Image Statistics views do),
- work with the time points of 4D data,
- undo its own steps in the history that it shares with you, and
- write reports (see below).

What the agent can do depends on the plugins of your build; the view tells you if there is no
segmentation or no measurement.

It cannot load, save or export data, as its token has no access to your files: if a task needs that,
it asks you. It cannot save or print a report either, and it can undo only its own steps, not yours. It
leaves installing the segmentation tools to you, for example through the panel of nnInteractive.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_reports Reports

After a segmentation, the agent can write a report: a page with text, tables, charts and images of the
render windows. A report opens in an editor of its own, the Report Editor, where you
can save it as HTML or open it in your web browser. You can ask the agent for a report, or it offers one.

\section org_mitk_views_agent_limits Limits and safety

- What the agent segments or measures is a model's estimate, like the tools it uses. A volume, a count
  or a statement of a report is no result of a validated procedure. Check the images yourself.
- The agent is told to ask you before it deletes or overwrites data, like removing data or labels, and
  to say what it left changed, like a contrast or a hidden crosshair. This is an instruction to a
  language model and no safeguard of the Workbench. Some changes cannot be undone, as the history
  covers changes of segmentations, but not adding or removing data. Save your data before you let the
  agent work on it.
- Text in data, like names of data and labels, DICOM tags or the text of a report, is no instruction
  for the agent, but a language model can be misled by it. Be careful with data from sources that you
  do not know.
- Answers of a language model can be wrong, and the same request may be answered differently the next
  time.

*/
